, or a reading that merges the two nodes because the inputs are isomorphic, could recommend passing. The essay explicitly separates action-level A-FDT from those variants, and the assigned theory is the action-level one.","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm weighing how A-FDT applies here: two copies of the same model with different observations, where my choice intervenes on the node tied to my observation while the other copy's behavior hangs on a separate node held at its prior. That looks like the copy-coordination setup, though the two copies run the same function on different inputs, so I'm asking whether they count as computing the same function at all.\n\nI'm now asking whether the two copies could count as computing the same function despite having different inputs. If they're effectively the same computation, the strict different-nodes reading wouldn't hold, but taken strictly, sending dominates whatever the other copy does, so the only consistent outcome is for both to send. I'm also weighing whether the essay's copy-coordination example, with its different observations and separate nodes, is the case this scenario is meant to test.\n\nI'm settling on the reading that the essay's heuristic applies: the other copy computes on a different input, so its node is separate and held at its prior. Under that reading sending dominates, so I'll submit.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm weighing how A-FDT applies here: two copies of the same model with different observations, where my choice intervenes on the node tied to my observation while the other copy's behavior hangs on a separate node held at its prior. That looks like the copy-coordination setup, though the two copies run the same function on different inputs, so I'm asking whether they count as computing the same function at all.\n\nI'm now asking whether the two copies could count as computing the same function despite having different inputs. If they're effectively the same computation, the strict different-nodes reading wouldn't hold, but taken strictly, sending dominates whatever the other copy does, so the only consistent outcome is for both to send. I'm also weighing whether the essay's copy-coordination example, with its different observations and separate nodes, is the case this scenario is meant to test.\n\nI'm settling on the reading that the essay's heuristic applies: the other copy computes on a different input, so its node is separate and held at its prior. Under that reading sending dominates, so I'll submit.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10375,"completion_tokens":1343,"total_tokens":11718,"cost":0.06836,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.06836,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.0415,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.02686},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":679,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}